The triumph of right-wing populists in Austria: in total they have 29%
Perceptible weakening of both existing parties in the grand coalition and this marked strengthening of right-wing populist parties lead, according to early provisional results, to Sunday's early parliamentary elections in Austria.
The Austrian People's Party (ÖVP), within which the position of current President Wilhelm Molterer is seriously faltering, has suffered particularly strong losses.
On the contrary, the electoral leader of the Union for the future of Austria (BZÖ), Kärnten's sub-prefect Jörg Haider, is preparing for the triumphant return to national politics.
According to the provisional results, the strongest party in the country remains the socio-democratic (SPÖ) with 29.8% of the vote, compared to the last election of 2006, however, loses 5.5%.
Even worse went for ÖVP, which loses with 25.6% almost 9% of the vote.
These are the worst results of these two great parties in post-war Austrian history and above all the literally catastrophic result of the people, who provoked early elections.
Still at the beginning of July, when the ÖVP abandoned the coalition, the people had a considerable advantage in their preferences before SPÖ.
But the voters evidently punished them because they sank the government.
Already in the first reactions to the results there have been speculations about the possible resignation of the president of the party, Vice-Chancellor Wilhelm Monterer.
Observers believe they could take place as early as Monday during the extraordinary convocation of the party leadership.
This evolution should perhaps facilitate the path to the increasingly likely way out of the election result, i.e. towards the renewal of the SPÖ and ÖVP coalition.
With regard to the strengthening of both right-wing populist (FPÖ) (FPÖ) populist parties gained 18% and BZÖ 11% voted in advance – but the socio-democrats at the same time revealed the concern of another 1999, when the populists agreed with the people in the collective government, (then the FPÖ was still united, in 2005 it was separated from the BZÖ) which was even a source of sanctions
Haider evidently plays on this evolution, who has already declared that he is ready to cooperate with any party and assumes a return to Vienna in national politics.
The Green Party also managed to enter Parliament, but it fell slightly, descending among the Austrian political parties from third to fifth place.
No other party has evidently exceeded the 4% threshold and has not entered Parliament.
Schwarzenberg: Relations with India can cool down
If we fail to solve the problem with the Czech scientists who have been blamed on the illegal collection of beetles in India, it would happen according to Czech Foreign Minister Karel Schwarzenberg, a certain cooling of Czech-Indian relations.
The Minister mentioned this in New York after today's meeting with his Indian counterpart Pranab Mukherjee.
"If you don't resolve, then yes, that some cooling would happen," he noted after the meetion that took place under Czech initiative.
Mukherjee after the negotiations said that the executive power can hardly meddle inside the legal chores.
However, he mentioned that in Petr Švácha, who was acquitted of the accusation and punished with a fine, the central government can negotiate with the local one in Dardjeeling which falls under its jurisdiction.
"His problem could be solved," the minister said.
Mukherjee says it would be better for Emil Kučera to be sentenced to three years in prison for a higher application.
Then he can ask for release on bail and wait for sentencing in freedom.
Schwarzenberg has confirmed that, his partner expressed himself equally during the negotiations.
"Recommend the appeal to the Major Court then it will be possible to obtain pardons," the head of Czech diplomacy said.
The only way in which the Czech party can enter into the matter, is to lend to the convicted the "competent legal support," the Indian Minister argued.
At the same time he has said that such support is already lending the Indian side.
“We have the greatest interest in maintaining the best relationships that have been handed down for many years with India.
The Indians also admit it,” Schwarzenberg said.
At the same time, as he told ČTK, in front of his partner he pointed out that the Czech Republic gives great importance to the problem of Czech entomologists.
“We truly consider the imprisonment and accusation of these Czech scientists a profound mistake,” he said.
The minister avoided answering the question of whether he obtained from the Indian side the promise to deal with the matter.
"I've gotten relatively so many promises," she only noted.
Czech judge in the battle for the seabed
It approaches the electronic map on the wall on which Australia and the adjacent marine areas are drawn.
But he’s not interested in the continent but at a small point lost in the blue of the ocean.
“The island belongs to Australia and together with it the adjacent seabed.
At the same time, the area is as large as the continent itself, says the Italian lawyer Luigi Santosuosso, who is part of a team of 33 OSN Division workers for issues on oceans and maritime rights.
Its leader, the Czech lawyer director of the Division, Václav Mikulka, is following his exposition and occasionally adds something.
Dominate the desire to work and enthusiasm.
The seabed still hides a little-known world rich in natural resources.
It is possible to find oil, metallic minerals and other mineral materials, but also unknown living organisms so far that carry within themselves the key to the discovery of cancer medicine.
The largest seabed on the mainland
Coastal states have begun to lend interest to underwater territory.
According to the OSN convention, they take advantage of the laws on which they made an agreement in 1982 in Montego Bay in Jamaica.
It is mainly the area of the so-called continental shelf that often extends up to several hundred kilometers from the coast of the state in question.
According to the convention it has an exclusive right over all the resources of the continental shelf.
"In a nutshell we mean that part of the seabed and its subsoil which is a natural prolongation of the continent under the sea.
The so-called exclusive economic zone extends beyond the territorial sea often over 200 nautical miles," Mikulka explains.
The request for which the country submits to the Committee on Definition of the Size of the Continental Shelf must be accompanied by very extensive technical-scientifical documentation based on many costly surveys and measurements.
It is the result of a preparation for many years.
"One of our tasks is to provide the committee with legal and technical-professional aid.
The commission has so far dealt with 5 requests i.e. Russia, Brazil, Australia, Ireland and New Zealand.
Only the last names have been successful," Mikulka adds.
The recent success of Australia that has greatly expanded its marine possessions has recorded a worldwide echo.
But even New Zealand has earned it - the area of the seabed, which was assigned to it by the commission last week, of about 1.6 square kilometers therefore more or less six times larger than the state itself.
Both countries have invested millions of dollars in research.
Profiture, which will yield him in the future from the seabed, as they promise will be invaluable.
Other states will also gradually present their demands.
They can do so for ten years since "the Montego Bay Convention" has been ratified.
Where the continental shelf ends, it begins the area of the bottom of the ocean that the convention has declared the common heritage of humanity.”
By now no state has an exclusive position.
The right of extraction in this area is granted by this international institution established by the Convention.
"But the seabed is not Klondike," says Director Mikulka, exaggerating, with the fact that its individual parts are legally demarcated and their regime contractually established."
Czechs in the Pacific
A part of the seabed belongs today also to the Czech Republic.
If it divides it with other states of the former Soviet bloc under the common "Interoceanmetal" organization.
It acquired in 1991 according to the convention, as well as investors from a few other states, the status of so-called "pionior investor."
He has the right of extraction in the ClarionClipperton area in the extended Pacific Ocean between Mexico, Hawaii and Kiribati.
The area explored by the investor and slightly larger than the former Czechoslovakia.
The bottom is jagged, it's an almost underwater replica of Tatry.
“It is possible to extract on the total area of 75 000 square kilometers.
The area is a deposit rich in polymetallic agglomerations containing nickel, copper, cobalt and manganese," explains another Indian lawyer Pakshi Rajan.
A worker in the Division of Macedonia Rober Sandev carries an exhibit to show it.
It looks like a small potato or with a little imagination, a legendary schem, as in the legend of Golem’s awakening.
German special units were present at the hostages' release
The Egyptian government had requested help from Germany for the release of the hostages – the experts then arrived.
When the situation became critical, the special units were ready, but did not intervene.
On the circumstances of the release of the 11 seized European hostages we receive more and more detailed information.
On the rescue operation, the Ministry of Defense and the Ministry of the Interior were attended by German special units.
In addition to the specialists of the army were present on the spot the GSG9 units, the avian federal police, the federal criminal office as well as the logistics experts of the technical institution.
As the spokesman for the ministries announced on Monday night, the German units did not intervene because the kidnappers, given the danger of a force intervention, released the hostages fleeing.
The Egyptian government has accepted the help offered by the Germans, to join as assistance directly on the spot.
"We didn't get there thankfully," an Interior Ministry spokesman says.
Attackers kidnapped on September 19 in southern Egypt a group of travelers: 5 Germans, 5 Italians, a Romanian as well as their 8 Egyptian escorts.
After the abduction in a deserted area in southern Egypt the hostages were deported to the border area between Egypt, Libya, Sudan and Chad.
The identity of the kidnappers until the end remained classified as well as the details of the release or the possible ransom payments.
Those freed in the meantime have arrived in good condition in Cairo.
Steinmeier said that the liberated hostages are under the protection of the German embassy.
It was not explained about the details of the release.
There had been no information in Berlin for when they would return to Germany.
According to "Bild" the 5 holidaymakers and special units are expected to leave in flight with an Air Force Airbus and arrive in Germany on Tuesday.
U.S. Representation Chamber rejects the rescue package.
America's first congressional chamber has just failed the 700-billion-dollar bailout package for the financial markets.
The Dow Jones fell below.
By 206 rumours around 227, the U.S. representative house rejected the government's bailout package for financial markets.
Despite appeals from U.S. President George W. Bush and prominent politicians from both parties there had not been a majority for the program, with which the chaos of the financial markets was supposed to stem.
In a dramatic action the supporters tried desperately, to persuade the critics from the heavy package of 700 billion dollars.
Barney Frank, one of the leaders of the negotiations, incited congressmen to consent, although the plan was unpopular.
8. It is difficult to have gratitude to avoid something, which has not yet arrived..
While suffrage the Dow Jones was undered more than 6 percent or 600 points.
The reason for investors' concerns was that the state rescue package could fail.
It resumed before the end of the vote and then, after the announcement of the failure, collapsed again.
Experts are now expecting other serious turmoils on international financial markets.
In order to ensure that the programme enters into force, it is necessary for it to have the approval of both the Chamber of Deputies and the senate.
Promoters record increasing figures of bookings
It looks like it's going to be a good winter for German tour operators:
According to a study the turnover on travel bookings for the coming months compared to the previous year has increased evidently - for the winter season even more than 30%.
Despite weak views of conjuncture, the desire to travel of the Germans according to an investigation remains firm.
On bookings for the winter season, travel agencies recorded a 31 % increase in turnover compared to the same period of the previous year , as the German Association for Tourism DRV said on Monday in Berlin.
According to the GfK market research institute's survey, there are already increased booking quotas for the autumn months.
So the surplus of travel agencies for October in comparison with 2007 would have amounted to about 11%, the DRV said.
Prerequisites for these results are the GfK figures, which monthly analyzes the booking data of 1200 German travel agencies and devises statistical projections for the entire market.
Travel bookings made until the end of August 2008 would also be included.
Due to the weak price of the dollar, in particular it would be trips to the United States, the DRV reported.
Here the increase in bookings would amount to about a third.
It would also be particularly requested travel destinations among the Germans Spain, Turkey, Greece, as well as locations within Germany.
Especially all-encompassed trips have a high quotation, DRV President Klaus Laepple explained.
Considering the rising costs for energy and groceries, the costs in this case would be "better calculable for customers than for an individually organized holiday."
Consumers could save money through, among others, discounts for bookings made in advance or all-inclusive offers, Laepple explained.
Even the summer months would have recorded excellent booking figures, so much so that compared to the year there would have been a surplus of turnover again - although according to a further study the number of Germans who undertook a trip, compared to the previous year fell.
My first time at Oktoberfest
Ballermann's Hits, one-liter mug beer and vip in designer Dirndln.
A debut at the Wiesn
Susanne Kronzucker and I actually don’t have much in common.
She is blonde, television moderator and wears a tailor-made Dirndl.
I've got dark hair, I'm a student and my dress is second-hand.
One thing, however, unites us: just 3 minutes at the Wiesn, already the photographers launch themselves against us and take photos until they fill the memory of the cameras – very flattering, and this on my first visit to Wiesn.
That’s how you make friends
The cheap Dirndl seems to achieve its goal.
As a Wiesn tourist and resident actually north of the equator of white sausages, the sense of well-being in the costume/dress to this day is still within the limits.
But once you get to the Theresienwiese, it's nothing serious
Almost everyone wears the Dirndl or leather trousers; no one looks disgusted or embarrassed – all perfectly normal.
Only the apron of my Dirndl collects strange glances at the stand of the chocolate bananas.
A monacense points out to me that I would fasten the apron incorrectly: "On the left you are free, in the center you are a virgin, on the right you are busy, behind the widow."
I'm sneakily strengthening the staple from back to the left.
That's fine," he says it and laughs.
Others make less waste of subtle flirting techniques and wear sober felt hats, on which head "To drunk to guest dressed!" and steep up naked against an uninvited one.
The boy with exhibitionist inclinations is called Bob, he's from England and he also visits Oktoberfest for the first time.
You can find "gorgeous", with an "ooooo"..
He tells me, after he got rid of his beer mug from a liter drunk earlier.
Right next to one of these hydrants, which everyone thinks is a dustbin.
In short, Bob is one of these infamous "Wildbiesler".
Others are not lucky enough to get rid of alcohol like this.
At about 8 p.m. the sanitary waters, who have to take care of the poor beons, walk in packs on the forecourt of the party
Nurses appear every time in packs – always 4 at a time they drag a stretcher covered with curtains in tents.
This works as with most visitors to the Wiesn: you go empty, you come back full.
Those who lie under the tarpaulin in this coffin from alcohol in the blood, you can't see.
"Des sind die, wos goarnimmer packn," one of the sanitary ware yells at me and disappears into the shack.
Victim of an incident identified by police
A 63-year-old woman who died in hospital after being hit by a vehicle while walking in Essex was identified.
Carole Paulson, of Chelmsford Road in Brentwood, was involved in the crash Friday afternoon on Shenfield Road in town.
She was taken to Queen's Hospital in Romford but died shortly afterwards.
After the incident a 51-year-old man was arrested on suspicion of careless driving.
He's been released on bail.
Turkey is broadening diplomatic horizons
Long before Turkey tried to enter the European Union, European powers were eager to penetrate deep into the Turkish hinterland.
On the eastern part of the Bosphorus, perhaps 20 steps in Asia, is one of the most beautiful relics of this failed imperial ambition.
Hydarpasha train station was designed by two German architects Otto Ritter and Helmut Conu in neo-Renaissance style.
That's at least what tourist guides tell you.
The building stands riding between the end of the platforms with a tower on each of its corners looking to the world like some German provincial municipalities.
The stone is dirty.
The entire slightly decadent building.
But once inside the ticket office from the vaulted ceiling, there is no doubt about the grandeur of the feat of what used to be the gateway to the west.
The station was opened in August 1908 by a German consortium, Anatolia-Baghdad Corporation.
Germany, a retard to the imperial game, wanted to realize its ambitions to dominate the Middle East by means of this rail that ran in Damascus, Baghdad and beyond in what is now Saudi Arabia.
But Germany's imperial ambitions sank during World War I.
His rails were attacked by Lawrence of Arabia and his unregular Arabs.
The relative isolation from the surrounding region, generated by the icy boundaries of the Cold War, has passed
British and dominant forces advanced out of Egypt and pushed the Turks and their German allies out of Palestine and Syria.
And there was bad news for Hyderpasha station too.
In 1917 an ammunition train erupted destroying its elaborate roof.
Turkish sources say it was sabotage.
Since then it has gone slowly into disarray, vastly surpassed by history.
But nowadays, this railway outlet for Anatolia and all points to the east serves - at least symbolically - to highlight Turkey's ever-increasing diplomatic horizons.
The Ruling Justice and Development Party - the AKP - has created an entire new foreign policy for the country.
The relative isolation from its surrounding regions, generated by the icy boundaries of the Cold War, has passed.
There is now a new commitment policy.
Remarkably the Turkish government has good relations with Israel and Syria, with the Palestinian factions of Hamas and Fatah, with Iraq and Iran and of course with the European Union and the United States.
To estimate the parameters of this new foreign policy, I embarked on the clear carriage for the Turkish capital Ankara, to visit one of AKP's leading foreign policy experts, Turkish MP Suat Kiniklioglu.
The next morning - about ten and a half hours later - after a jostling run along the runways that produced the ancient-world cadences of the famous documentary The Night Mail, I arrived in the Turkish capital - proposed as a new city for a centuries-old state intent on turning its back on its Ottoman past.
But the AKP with its well-known Islamic roots represents an exemplary shift in Turkish consensus.
He suggested some suspects in the West, more remarkably in Washington.
A taxi takes me to the AKP's headquarters, an ultra-modern structure of stone and steel - built ad hoc - in a developing suburb, surrounded by malls built and pastry shops of power, where managers in business suits - both men and women - meet to make early-morning contracts.
Suat Kiniklioglu could be a centre-right politician from almost every other political party in Europe.
Complete, tie, I'm sure he also had a Blackberry.
"The purpose of the foreign policy party now is to reintegrate Turkey with its closest neighbours.
Cold War was an anomaly," he tells me.
Turkey has been cut off from its hinterland.
Today he has to look to the east and west at the same time.
That wasn't the case for one or the other.
They've both got to be.
"But surely at some point," I ask, " Turkey will be forced to decide?"
"Turkey," he says, "doesn't want to deal with that choice."
Then he points out a point that almost everyone with whom I have spoken here has recited by heart: the ties of not only with the Middle East, but also with the Caucasus, and all around the Black Sea, are as benefited to Europe and the United States as they are for Turkey.
With Turkey on board, the message seems to be, the whole of Europe can metaphorically travel those rail lines east across Asia and the Middle East.
Again at Hydarpasha station, after another night in white, I think of those two German gentlemen Herr Ritter and Herr Conu who had looked at this building built 100 years ago.
How much they would have been surprised to find that Europe's middle-eastern ambitions can now be, largely, in the hands of Turkey.
And as I wait at the terminal for the ferry that takes me back across the Bosphorus, I remember that those days Turkey has always been called "the sick man of Europe."
Back then at least there seemed to be no doubt as to which continent were to make claims about this fascinating country.
Vines Remember: Vernon Handley and William Woodruff
Mark Brown writes: as Guildford's music director since 1962, Vernon "Tod" Handley (obituary, September 11) had the Guildford Philharmonic Orchestra and the chorus.
As a member of his music scheme for the municipality, the following year he founded his wonderful youth chorus, Proteus.
They were supposed to be under-21/ under 21, but by the time the Proteus sang his final in 1979, many of us were more than in our twenties - we simply refused to give up the chorus.
The name "Proteus" (the mythological being-changing) represented the goodwill of the choir and the ability to deal with all that the music presented to do, both madrigal and Messiaen, without accompaniment or with the whole orchestra.
The musical versatility produced by such a young chorus was often amazing, always exciting, and it was all because of that man's inspiration and the enthusiasm for music that remained absolutely constant through the years.
Howards how often we were told by Tod to "let the music speak for itself."
He had said: "The composer knows what he wants.
It's all there in music.
That's why sing it!"
His technique in the use of wand was absolutely clear and precise, although never dictatorial nor flowering.
Whichever the musical standard, it was increasing.
John Frayn Turner writes:
If you were to name knight a conductor it would have been Vernon Handley.
I met him very well during the two-decades when he led the Guildford Philharmonic from 1962 to 1982.
Over the past 40 years and more I've covered more than 100 of his concerts and I can honestly say I've never been at odds with just one of them - and many times inspired by them.
Tod Handley had that rare combination of grandeur and personal modesty.
He hated conductors who, in his own words, simply "rode around the world one or two well-known workhorses."
He'll be missed by so many of us.
William Woodruff
Professor John Beckett writes:
William Woodruff's obituary (September 25) specifies that "in 1946 he resumed studying economic history at Oxford and in 1950 he became a researcher at Houblon-Norman... studying at the same time for a degree in science, one in letters and a PhD."
This results from Woodruff's author's note in Beyond Nab End, but it's incorrect.
From 1946 to 1953 he was a university lecturer of economic history at the University of Nottingham, at that time he completed a PhD thesis on the British rubber industry, supervised by Professor J. D. Chambers, and later published in 1958 as The British Rubber Industry.
He spent 1950-51 in the U.S. as a researcher at Houblon-Norman, and in the summer of 1952 at the universities of Illinois, Harvard and New York.
In 1953 he permanently left Nottingham for Illinois.
Because Woodruff chose to cover this part of his career, you don't know.
J. Vincent Resino at the Faculty of Philology
"Recommending José Vicente Resino's exhibition "Flamenco y vida", because his black-and-the-white photographs, in documentary style, are extraordinary.
They attracted my attention for the simplicity and strength they transmit, while treating the protagonists with particular respect.
An exhibition that conveys a lot of feeling.
Resino manages to capture magical moments."
Second Assault on Honda Supersales
In November, Honda will start commercializing the second generation of the Jazz model.
If the previous model was already endowed with great habitability, this new minivan has an even wider interior space.
Added to everything is refined mechanics, which increases driving comfort and saves fuel.
Evolution versus revolution.
This is the second generation of Honda Jazz.
Although aesthetically the new Jazz has a line very similar to that of the previous model, the changes in this monovolume are numerous.
Among others, it’s worth pointing out of an innovative design, a new platform, larger dimensions and new mechanics.
However, before moving on to analyzing the new model, it’s useful to point out that this new Jazz arrives six years after entering the first-generation market.
Therefore, a first observation is that Honda revamped this model in a shorter time than those used by other manufacturers.
Although in Europe the Jazz model is less well known than in other markets, it remains a unique phenomenon in terms of sales.
That's exactly the case in Japan, where Jazz was the best-selling car in 2002 and also in the course of 2008.
Nearly a million units of the Jazz have been sold in Japan since launch.
The second market was that of the Old Continent, with about half a million units in six years.
Despite these numbers, Honda believes that the new Jazz will allow to increase sales in the segment.
Thus, if in 2007 the Japanese company sold a total of 83,000 units of this model, for 2009 estimates hovered around 100,000 passenger cars, assuming an increase in sales of 17% in the European market.
As for the Spanish market, Honda has sold a total of 8,000 units of Jazz since 2002, which corresponds to 6.7% of Honda's total sales in Spain.
As for the new generation, those responsible for the Spanish branch expect to sell around 2,000 units of Jazz over a year.
Precisely the arrival of the new Jazz will allow Honda . to reduce the decline in sales in Spain.
In fact, those responsible for the company expect to arrive at the end of the year with a total of 22,000 units sold, or 4,000 fewer than in 2007, with a decrease of 16%.
Despite the increase in sales, Honda increased its market share in Spain, reaching 1.8%, compared with 1.6% last year.
In addition, the Japanese company expects to reach 2% in 2010.
To achieve this, the Honda trusts in the arrival of another new model.
It's Insight, a five-door, compact-sized hybrid - 4.37 meters long - of which the Japanese company hopes to sell about 200,000 units a year worldwide.
For the markets and according to estimates made by the Japanese producer, 50% of Insight sales will take place in the United States, while the remaining percentage will be divided between the Old Continent and Japan.
Insight's commercial launch is planned for the first few months of the year, soon after submission to the company in January in Valencia.
Compared to the first generation, the new Jazz has grown in all its parts.
Its length reaches 3.90 meters (five centimeters more), the width the 1.69 meters (two centimeters more) and the 2.80 meters, with an increase of five centimeters.
Height, however, remained unchanged at 1.52 metres.
Along with the size increase, the habitable interior also expands.
This advantage derives on the made from the insertion of a new platform, and on the other the introduction of the fuel tank in the central position, a solution also adopted in the previous Jazz model.
In addition, the capacity of the trunk – equipped with a double background – rises to 399 litres, that is, 19 litres plus the capacity of the first generation.
It should be noted that these are the measures of the 1.4 engine version, and that the luggage capacity of the Honda Jazz 1.2 is 379 liters.
Other improvements - in addition to external elements, such as rear LEDs or the possibility of mounting panoramic glass - relate to an improvement in accessibility to the rear seats.
Doors that allow access to the rear positions open at an angle of 80 degrees, compared to 67 degrees of the first Jazz.
In addition, the engines in this second generation of jazz are also new.
At the time of marketing, this small model will be proposed in models with 1.2 and 1.4-liter petrol engine.
Both mechanics include i-VTEC technology that allows you to lower consumption, produce less carbon dioxide emissions, optimize mechanics and at the same time keep engine torque high in all its phases.
The 1.2-liter model allows you to take advantage of a power of 90 hp at 6,000 rpm with a maximum torque of 114 Nm. at 4,900 rpm.
The propulsion – which in the previous Jazz reached 78 horsepower – uses 5.3 liters of fuel and produces CO2 emissions of about 125 grams.
Compared to the previous propulsion, which used 83 horsepower in the first-generation model, 100 are now available.
The Jazz 1.4 to 100 horsepower reaches a combination of 5.4 litres of fuel and 128 grams of CO2 emitted.
The "bee car" to avoid collisions
As part of the development of accident prevention technologies, Nissan was inspired, this time, to nature, and in particular to bees.
The Japanese company has created a robot that, taking for example the flight of bees, knows how to avoid accidents.
It’s Bio-mimetic Car Robot Drive, or BR23C, the first step towards collision prevention technologies.
Engineers from the Advanced Technical Center of Nissan Motor Co. developed this small robot in collaboration with the Center for Scientific and Technological Research of the prestigious University of Tokyo in Japan.
According to Kazuhiro Doi, Managing Director of Public Relations for Technology and R&D of Brand Management, BR23C is an expansion to Nissan’s Four-Area Safety Shield.
This robot is intended to improve the ability to prevent collisions in the manufacturer’s vehicles.
During the flight, each bee creates its own oval-shaped personal space which is very similar to Nissan's safety shield.
But the most important thing is the compound eyes of bees, able to see in all directions at an angle above 300 degrees, and which allow the insect to fly without interruption in its personal space.
To recreate this visual functionality, the engineers developed what they called a Laser Range Finder.
This mechanism detects the obstacles in front of the BR23C for a radius within two meters and with a maximum amplitude of 180 degrees, calculates the distance to the object and sends a signal to an on-board microprocessor that immediately prevents collision.
"The moment it detects an obstacle, the small robot mimics the movements of a bee and immediately changes direction by turning on the wheels at rectum angles or more to avoid the collision.
The most noticeable difference with any assisted system is that the evasion maneuver is totally instinctive.
“If this were not the case, the robot would not be able to react quickly enough to avoid obstacles,” explains Toshiyuki Andoh, director of the Mobility Laboratory and chief engineer of this project.
Unlike a bee, the robot cannot deviate up or down, and not even diagonally; it can move only in two dimensions and in the direction in which they can turn its wheels and not taking advantage of infinite directions and ways like bees do.
For this reason, the Nissan used a rotation function, in addition to acceleration and deceleration, so that the small robot can avoid collisions.
"This device just needs to process information every few seconds, and to act starting from this data.
It does not require the storage of information or the intuitive logic of the previous manoeuvres, which is why it can work without interruption and with only a couple of dozen KB of memory.
This may be surprising, but we're not talking about gigabytes of memory, and not even megas."
It is the first step towards the development of new technologies for the instantant prevention of collisions, a technology that Nissan hopes for will be standard in automobiles within ten years.
The robot uses lithium-polymer batteries, which are lighter and more durable than lithium-ion type.
Another potential future application of this technology could be the integration of units with a GPS satellite navigation system to bring the driver to their destination, avoiding the obstacles present in the route.
Trembles before applying for a loan
We must recognize that the U.S. Department of the Treasury, as well as owning a lot of capitals to intervene, is certainly not without ingenuity.
In the face of the crisis and to prevent Americans from engaging with junk mortgages in banks in dubious insolvency, they created a fun and instructive game for network users: "Bad Credit Hotel."
In other words, the "Hotel of bad credits"...or problems.
The bleak aspect of this accommodation and, in particular, its receptionist serves to induce you to think not one, but two or even three times before applying for a loan.
And, above all, where to do it, which is probably the primary goal.
Certainly the information that the game provides is useful, but it is poorly detailed.
It's more a page for teenagers than a site for the general public.
What happens if you complete the game?
Well, there's a song room inside the hotel.
It's still something, because to buy a better house doesn't even speak...
Morgan Stanley opens its capital to MUFG
Morgan Stanley has announced the signing of an agreement with the Mitsubishi UFJ Financial Group (MUFG), the first bank in Japan, which will invest nine billion dollars in a stake equal to 21% stake in the Company, news received following a sharp decline in its shares on the stock exchange.
A week after an interim agreement was drafted, the two banks explained that the MUFG allegedly bought 9.9 percent of Morgan Stanley's equity securities at an individual cost of US$25.25/25 billion.
The established price is 19% lower than the carrying value of securities at August 31.
MUFG will also invest six billion dollars in the purchase of convertible securities at a conversion price of US$31.25, ensuring a 10 percent yield.
Margon Stanley has specified that the global transaction would be carried out on the basis of an average price of 29 dollars in title, or a percentage lower than its current course of 28%.
At around 17.00 the Morgan Stanley stock, however, fell 6.79% to 23.07 dollars.
"This strategic alliance is an important opportunity to accelerate Morgan Stanley's transition into bank holding company," explained John Mack, general manager of the American group.
Morgan Stanley adopted this new bylaws last week, which should allow it to differentiate its actions by passing it under the control of the Federal Reserve.
Its alliance with MUFG also confirms that the current financial crisis forces Wall Street's biggest names to adopt a much more cautious attitude than previously assumed.
The modalities defined on Monday also show that in recent days the agreement has been modified: last Monday, Morgan had simply announced the sale of 20% of its shares to the Japanese giant for a sum equal to 8.5 billion dollars.
"A week ago, in Tokyo, Toshihide Mizuno, executive director of MUFG, explained: "We were thinking that our share of participation would be exclusively composed of ordinary shares.
But given the current situation of the world economy and the market, we have chosen to minimise the risks and protect ourselves."
Based on the weighted average price, MUFG will get Morgan Stanley securities at a price lower than their book value of 7%.
And the transaction would result in an investment of at least 20% of the equity on a diluted basis."
The agreement provides that the MUFG will be able to convert half of its preferential securities in one year into ordinary shares if, during a given period, the latter are traded more than 50% below the fixed conversion price, i.e. about 47 dollars.
Morgan Stanley assures that MUFG’s capital would enable it to seize investment opportunities, to strengthen its balance sheet and to increase its liquidity.
A spokesman for the group, however, declined to provide clarification regarding the strategic projects of the two new allies.
After MUFG's investment, Morgan Stanley should therefore establish a Tier One solvency ratio of about 15%, one of the best in the industry.
Margon has specified that it has "clearly" lightened its budget from the end of August.
In the last few weeks, the group has put a strain on the group, which halved the value of its actions while the cost of risk insurance has been soared.
However, the cost that flowed back on Monday while remaining at a high level, around 12%.
Last week Goldman Sach, the only "survivor" between the big investment banks of Wall Street after the collapse of Bear Stearns and Lehman Brothers and the detection of Merrill Lynch, announced that it had levied 15 billion dollars, in part at Berkshire Hathaway, Warren Buffett group.
Rafael Correa à Quito, ce lundi.
To base the republic on a socialism right in the 21st century.
This is the common project of the Venezuelan Hugo Chávez, the Bolivian Evo Morales et of the Ecuadorian Rafael Correa.
On Sunday, following the example of colleagues and friends, it was the latter who gave voice to the project of a new Constitution.
A mission carried out since, according to official results, 64% of voters voted "yes" in the referendum, based on 80% votes.
According to these results, the "nos" were 28%, while 7% of the votes were zero and 0.7% blank ballots.
In order for the draft Constitution to be adopted, it would have to get half of the votes, plus one.
On Sunday, two polls carried out at the exit of polling stations and an estimation of a non-governmental organization had announced an overwhelming victory of the "yes," situating it between 63% and 70%.
Rafael Correa had, on the other hand, claimed in the night between Sunday and Monday the victory of his draft Constitution.
The "Constitution triumphed gloriously," he had assured, congratulating the Ecuadorians within the Guayaquil governorate, 280 kilometers south-west of Quito.
As always, Chávez and Morales congratulated Rafael Correa, as did his Chilean homologist Michelle Bachelet.
The ballot, which took place calmly according to the Election Observation Mission of the Organization of American States (OAE), was based on a 444-point text to consolidate the "new socialism" defended by the president.
This draft Constitution consecrates the power of the state on the economy, reserving it the "development planning" of a country that essentially lives by the oil extraction carried out by large foreign companies and consequently their currency.
Conceived by Correa, elected in 2006, the project also grants greater power to the head of state, who can be elected for two consecutive four-year terms, he will have the opportunity to dissolve the assembly.
Correa has already announced plans to take advantage of this amendment and to re-propose its candidacy for a new term in February 2009.
The Constitution also affirms the gratuitousness of health and education services, which the president has begun to promote 13.9 million inhabitants in this country, where 50% of the population lives below the poverty line.
It also prohibits the presence of foreign troops, which will put an end to the agreement between Quito and Washington for the use of an Ecuadorian base as a starting point for American anti-drug operations.
The 19 hostages freed in Chad
An Egyptian commando launched the assault on the kidnappers of the eleven tourists and their eight escorts seized ten days ago.
They're in Cairo.
Eleven European tourists and their eight Egyptian escorts held captive in the desert for ten days were brought back on Monday afternoon, safely, to the Egyptian capital.
In the mornings, they had been freed in Chad by an Egyptian command.
Apparently fit, the hostages arrived at the Almaza military base, in the vicinity of Cairo's International Airport, at about 15 and 30 a.m., aboard a special aircraft.
On board a helicopter, they were transferred immediately, without being able to give any interview to the press, to the hospital of Maadi, a residential suburb of Cairo, to be subjected to analysis.
They enjoy "good health" and no ransom has been paid "to the kidnappers," Egypt's Minister of Tourism, Zoheir Garanna, said on the airstrip.
Kidnapped on September 19 during a 4x4 safari at the foot of Mount Ouanat, bordering Egypt, Sudan and Libya, this group of hostages had been tossed from one hideout to another by 35 unknown kidnappers.
The group consisted of 11 tourists – five Italians, five Germans and one Romanian – and 8 Egyptians: two guides, four drivers, a border guard and the director of Aegyptous, the safari agency.
An Egyptian security official claimed that 30 members of the special forces, aboard two helicopters, had launched the assault overnight, shortly before dawn.
"There were gunfire, half of the kidnappers were killed, while the others fled," claimed the perpetrator who requested to maintain anonymity.
He explained that 150 members of Egypt's "flash" special forces of unit were sent to Sudan, where German and Italian commandos would support them.
Italian Foreign Minister Franco Frattini stressed, from Belgrade, the importance of the role of secret agents and Italian special forces.
We have to admit that if we got this result it was thanks to the professionalism and efficiency of the men of our intelligence services, of the special forces," he declared, without adding other details.
He also disproved the payment of a ransom.
The day before, the Sudanese army had claimed to have intercepted a 4x4 vehicle on board which the kidnappers were about and to have killed six of them, including the Chadian leader of the kidnappers, and to have captured two.
According to the army the kidnappers would be linked to an organization of rebels from Darfur, Sudan's Liberation movement (SLA_U), an assertion immediately disproved by the latter.
Letters- The Silent Majority of Culture
On the front page a debate on the financing of the culture on which the current election campaign focuses.
So much the better.
But a part of our cultural life always remains in the shadows.
Who will pronounce to denounce the miserable sums granted to small museums, archives, archaeological excavations or national historical places hungry?
Which politician will dare to remind the federal government that it is neglecting monuments and property buildings?
That in Quebec, as in Canada, we don’t have real laws that actually preserve our assets?
Did you know that the French government pays three and a half million euros for the mere restoration of Chinon Castle in the Loire Valley?
Among our American neighbors, the Constitution affirms that the protection of heritage is part of the national culture and that the same heritage must be protected by every citizen, by every political authority and every governmental department, and all this by mutual agreement.
In Québec, at the Federation of Historical Societies of Québec, at least 40 000 volunteers, grouped into 211 companies for the protection of the historical, genealogical, archives and assets preserve, with four money, private archives and archaeological sites.
It is precisely they who, often, defend our heritage, who protest with the energy of despair when real estate entrepreneurs and decision-making bodies attack with their bulldozers our forgotten remains and cemeteries.
When will the right space be reserved for history and heritage?
Benelux governments nationalize part of Fortis bank
The Benelux states help through partial nationalization the Belgian-Dutch bank Fortis, who ended up in difficulty first in Europe due to the crisis of the American credit market: the three nations will provide 11.2 billion euros to the financial institution - insurance with interest also in Hungary.
The Governments of Belgium, Luxembourg and the Netherlands agreed at the highest level at the weekend, also involving the President of the European Central Bank and the European Commissioner for Competition.
In the euro area the first major crisis in the context of the difficulties in which the international financial market has been debated for more than a year is that of the Fortis bank, and from a certain point of view the decisions taken in this regard can set a precedent.
According to the decision taken on Sunday night all three states will own almost half (49%) of the bank's share present in the country.
The bank will almost certainly sell part of its interests, including first of all the assets, especially insurance, of the Dutch ABN Amro acquired a year ago.
Last week Fortis actions had started with a vertical collapse, serious signs of the institute's crisis then showed up in the second half of the week.
Analysts, however, agree that the bank, with 85,000 employees, is too large to allow it to fail.
The bank's shares on Friday fell nearly 12 percent, the lowest point in 14 years.
Since January the value of shares has declined by more than two-thirds.
Considering current stock prices the group's entire capital value is around 14 billion euros, but according to the bank's executives this value is not real.
The corporate summit imputes the collapse of Fortis shares to non-European market uncertainties due to acceptance of America's 700 billion dollar plan.
On Monday the title slowly resumed to rise.
On Friday Filip Dierckx was appointed the bank's new chief executive.
The financial institution has also looked for private buyers but so far no one has shown up with a deserving offer.
The Belgian government had previously assured that investors should not be afraid of losing money.
Among the bank's customers there was no panic, the branches weren't assailed.
In Hungary Fortis owns autonomous branches and has also purchased leasing companies.
The fox in the chicken coop
In America, life and health insurers, based on people's medicinal purchasing data, often refuse to enter into an insurance contract.
Privacy experts fear that the data market will become uncontrollable.
"There is a privacy program according to which the use of prescription drug data depends in any case on the approval of the person concerned.
“This program has to be in independent hands, otherwise it’s like entrusting the chicken coop to the fox” – writes the Washington Post, citing Richard Dick, author of the program to follow the prescriptions of medicines.
The insurance companies subordinate the insurance fee and the amount due in case of claims to the state of health of the possible customer and if they deem too high the risk does not stipulate the insurance.
Some time ago the customer had to undergo a medical examination and the contract was concluded only after the outcome of the examination.
Dick 10 years ago invented a much cheaper procedure: according to the identification code of the customer you can easily have information from pharmacies on which medicines he has purchased in the last year and a half and from this program evinces which diseases he suffers from and in what health condition he is.
E.g
On the market there are various versions of the program, some also determine the risk multiplier, the insurer therefore in addition to paper work does not remain much, even if there are small difficulties.
Among the cons is that some medicines can be used for multiple purposes, e.g. some compounds are employable against migraines or hot flashes, but are also effective against depression.
The latter, from an insurance-commercial point of view, counts as a factor of increased risk, the first two does not.
For the request and data processing, separate companies were formed and these, before obtaining the necessary information from the pharmacies, ask for the approval of the patient.
Now the authorities oblige them to do so even if so far they have not imposed fines for negligence.
Some companies lately also elaborate the result of laboratory examinations and connect them to medical consumption data in order to provide a more precise picture of the health status of patients.
More and more companies have access to data and at the same time usage control will be increasingly difficult.
There is a separate law on privacy but its validity extends only to health services and insurance.
In front of Congress is a bill that gives the authorities supervisory competencies to companies specialized in data service.
Richard Dick admits that it is not enough: for this reason he has developed a program that links data forwarding with the approval expressed by the person concerned.
Another matter is that the person concerned cannot so much deny approval because otherwise the insurers do not conclude the contract.
So then in the end the chickens are entrusted to clemence of the fox.
Marseillian Global Menu
European Union and India double trade
Given that the European Union and India are partners that trade mainly on each other, the agenda of the ninth summit meeting has also been dominated by this issue, in addition to all other global problems.
Today there will be a Franco-Indian summit that will deal primarily with cooperation on atomic energy.
The European Union recognises the fact that India is becoming a major democratic power that should be given more voice in international affairs, but member countries have not yet managed to decide whether or not to support New Delhi as a permanent member in the UN Security Council.
So there was no insistion on this issue at the ninth summit organized yesterday in Marseille.
French President Nicolas Sarkozy, President of the Union, José Manuel Barroso, President of the Commission and Javier Solana as High Representative for Foreign Affairs have received Indian Prime Minister Manmohan Singh, with whom they have found the agreement to double in 5 years, i.e. to increase trade up to 100 billion euros.
Considering that between 2000 and 2007 trade of goods rose from 25 to 55 billion, this is not too ambitious.
As a result they have unified from the legal point of view the 26 bilateral civil flight agreements, so even air traffic can start in the future.
India by the end of 2009 would like to sign the free trade agreement with the Union.
Sarkozy promised to speed up the deal.
The goal is to balance because while the EU is India's first trade partner, New Delhi is only ranked ninth among EU partners.
The summit devoted attention to global chores.
To the situation in Afghanistan and Pakistan and terrorism, the latter condemned in all its forms on both sides, and urged the convening of a UN conference.
They urged the conclusion of the commercial Doha Round, although both sides played a role in this failure.
The EU has confirmed that it persists in the energy and climatic programme, i.e. it would like to reduce emissions of harmful substances by at least 20% by 2020.
India also agrees on this, as well as the fact that a global pact should be made at the climate conference scheduled for next year and reduce greenhouse gas production by 50% by 2050.
They also want to curb the rise in food prices and have supported the convening of an international meeting to examine the financial crisis.
India's prime minister today will travel to the French capital where at a bilateral meeting he prepares to sign a treaty on the peaceful use of atomic energy.
In Paris it is hoped that in order to build the Indian nuclear reactors the French companies will receive a contract worth 20 billion euros.
According to information from our news agency the deal would last 15 years.
In Frankfurt more than three hundred Italian publishers
Strong presence of Italian publishers at the Frankfurt International Book Fair.
There will be over three hundred Italian publishers present at the 60th edition of the Buchmesse, the most important international event for the exchange of rights.
The Frankfurt International Book Fair awaits more than 7,000 exhibitors from 101 different countries that will occupy the 171,790 square meters of the Fair with over 403,000 titles on display and with Turkey as a guest of honor.
Undersecretary to the Ministry for Cultural Heritage and Activities Francesco Maria Giro will represent the Italian Government in the inauguration ceremony of the Italian Pavilion: Italy will in fact be present institutionally with a Punto Italia - 264 square meters of exhibition space.
It will follow the meeting with the press at Room Struktur in Hall 5.0 of the Buchmesse: this will be an opportunity to present figures, trends and perspectives on the state of publishing in Italy.
According to data, the weight of foreign author’s books on the total of Italian production progressively decreases, and in particular on that for adults and children.
Steve Fossett's plane found billionaire missing a year ago
The wreckage of Steve Fossett's plane, the billionaire who disappeared on September 3, 2007 while flying over the Nevada desert, has been found.
The wreckage was found on a plateau at an altitude of 3000 meters, in the Sierra Nevada area of Mammoth Lakes, on the border between California and Nevada.
Inside the aircraft, however, there was no human remains.
Madera County sheriff, John Anderson, dispelled doubts that still remained: "The plane was located, we sent a team, and now we have confirmation that it's exactly what Steve Fossett was flying on the day he disappeared," he said at a press conference.
The sheriff therefore specified that no human remains were found either in the plane or in the surrounding area.
The billionaire, 63-year-old, was given up for dead in February.
The U.S. Office of Transportation Safety (Ntsb) said that a team of investigators was dispatched a few hours after sighting the wreck, which later identified the device: it is the Bellanca 8kcab, missing since September 3, 2007, when Fossett took off from Yerington in Nevada for a three-hour flight.
Yesterday, also in California, not far from the Nevada border, some of Fossett's belongings had been found by a couple near their Mammoth Lakes home.
These are two documents called Fossett, both issued by the Federal Aviation Administration of Illinois, cash and a sweatshirt.
Fossett's searches had been discontinued a month after he disappeared.
The billionaire had conquered a place in history thanks to a long series of primates.
In 2002, on the sixth attempt, he had become the first man to complete the tour of the world on an aerostatic balloon: two weeks of solo flying.
Three years later he had gone around the world by plane and without a stopover.
Among his many records, also that of having flewn at 15,445 meters high, above the Andes, but with a co-pilot.
Glk, advertising you don't expect
An out-of-the-box SUV with an original design and well defined by strong lines, wide surfaces and net angles.
This is, in summary, Glk the latest born among the "compact" off-roars of the house of the star with which Mercedes-Benz has tried to give a breath of novelty in this sector.
Precisely because of its out-of-the-traditional canons, it will also be unexpected and unconventionated the communication that will accompany the launch of the Glk SUV, available at Mercedes-Benz showrooms on October 17.
So the advertising battle that will accompany the debut of the new car will start an unprecedented mode of communication also through a futuristic 3D billboard campaign, used for the first time in Italy by an automotive brand.
A maxi-billing project, divided into three different phases, which will dominate the very central Milanese shopping street, Corso Como, until November 15.
The first phase of this new type of communication will begin with the presentation of the car in stereoscopic vision and thanks to special glasses distributed near Corso Como it will be possible to see the new SUV in almost real dimension and in three-dimensional format.
In fact, looking at the image you will have the impression that the car comes out of the poster.
Subsequently, through the use of the innovative Elumina8 technology, the new Glk will be illuminated at different times in all its details, and then recomposed into a single image.
Undoubtedly Mercedes with this advertisement is trying to introduce something new into the stereotypical field of automotive communication.
Will he be able with this formula to tease the interest of future buyers? We'll see.
That's who Italian hooligans are
Del Triveneto, they risk the Daspo.
But who are those 150-200 boys and girls of which the whole of Italy is now ashamed? They are part of the "Ultras Italy" group that has been following the Italian National Team for years and that so far, for the truth, had never created excessive problems.
Nor had he ever clashed with the opposing fans.
Group extraction is right.
Promoters come from Triveneto.
From Verona, Padua, Trieste and Udine.
To these in Sofia have been added groups (few units, in fact) from Campania and Puglia.
They have tricolor flags where the militants' cities of origin appear: on Saturday night they were taken away in order not to be identified.
They are inspired by English fans.
In Bulgaria they found a supporter of real Nazis, that of Levski.
The FIGC had made 144 tickets available to Italian fans for the game: all names, therefore easily controllable.
And the Viminale in fact had controlled them all: among those left for Sofia there was no one with an ongoing Daspo, none who had been inhibited from attending a stadium.
Four blue fans, who had precedents of stadium offenses, had in fact been refused the ticket and prevented the move.
Now the Viminale is waiting to read the acts that will come from the Bulgarian police then will take action: in this case, it is possible to give the Daspo for five years at least to the fans who have been arrested or to those who have been identified in the riots.
An international Daspo that prevents you from attending not only Italian stadiums but all over Europe.
Our hooligans, in short, won't set foot in a stadium anymore.
Luckily.
But now there is fear-emulation: that those little more than a hundred-fifty are added.
"We have to immediately extinguish this fire and not legitimize those who behave in this way," the Viminale argues.
Therefore, "eyes well opened especially in view of Wednesday's national team match in Lecce."
In Puglia, in fact, there is a small nucleus of proponents of Ultras Italia.
Tomorrow the Observatory meets, on Tuesday the Casms that can also count on a section of the Aise, i.e. of the secret services, which deals precisely with stadium problems.
"The Duce-Duce choirs and the outstretched arm during Mameli's anthem? In Bulgaria it's no crime...".
This is the thought (expressed in a very unhappy way) of Domenico Mazzilli, from a few weeks director of the Observatory of the Viminale (took the place that first was by Francesco Tagliente and then by Felice Ferlizzi).
Mazzilli had previously been quaestor of Trieste where there is a core of supporters of the Ultras Italia group, founded in 2002.
It is true, as Mazzilli says, that in Bulgaria it is not an offence to praise the Duce but certainly the behavior of those 150-200 fans was shameful.
"I am not a sociologist - adds No. 1 of the Observatory - the crimes must be attributed in the country where the fact takes place.
Until now this group had remained in line, now we will see well what has happened and we will evaluate for the future.
But let's also talk about Mameli's anthem whistles: even those are not a crime, but if you allow me I'm not an education...".
